Use <Dynkin labels> $w_i=\langle w,\alpha_i^\vee\rangle$, so the rows of the <Cartan matrix> are the <simple roots> expressed in <fundamental weight> coordinates. For the <A2 root system>,
$$
\boxed{A_{A_2}=\begin{pmatrix}2&-1\\-1&2\end{pmatrix},\qquad
\alpha_1=(2,-1),\quad\alpha_2=(-1,2).}
$$
Starting at the <highest weight> $(1,0)$ and subtracting successive allowed <simple roots> gives $(1,0)\to(-1,1)\to(0,-1)$. Negating these <weights> gives the <dual representation>. Hence the two fundamental <weight systems> are
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
\mathbf3=(1,0):&\quad (1,0),\ (-1,1),\ (0,-1),\\
\overline{\mathbf3}=(0,1):&\quad (0,1),\ (1,-1),\ (-1,0).
\end{aligned}}
$$
Each listed <weight> has <weight multiplicity> one. The <highest weight> $(1,1)=\alpha_1+\alpha_2$ is the <highest root>, so its <representation> is the <adjoint representation>. Its nonzero <weights> are the six <roots>, while the <Cartan subalgebra> supplies the zero <weight> twice:
$$
\boxed{\mathbf8=(1,1):\quad
\pm(2,-1),\quad\pm(-1,2),\quad\pm(1,1),\quad(0,0)^{[2]}.}
$$
The superscript $[2]$ indicates <weight multiplicity>. This also follows from $\mathbf3\otimes\overline{\mathbf3}=\mathbf8\oplus\mathbf1$: the nine <tensor product> <weights> comprise six differences of distinct fundamental <weights> and three zeros, one zero belonging to the <trivial representation>.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For an <A2 root system> <weight> $w=(w_1,w_2)^T$, the specified <electric charge> and <flavour hypercharge> are the linear functionals
$$
q(w)=\frac{2w_1+w_2}{3},\qquad y(w)=\frac{w_1+2w_2}{3}.
$$
Apply these to the <meson octet> <weights> in <3/a/solution>. The results, with <electric charges> measured in units of the positive elementary <electric charge>, are
$$
\boxed{\begin{array}{c|rr|c}
w&q&y&\text{pseudoscalar octet member}\\\hline
(2,-1)&1&0&\pi^+\\
(-2,1)&-1&0&\pi^-\\
(1,1)&1&1&K^+\\
(-1,2)&0&1&K^0\\
(-1,-1)&-1&-1&K^-\\
(1,-2)&0&-1&\bar K^0\\
(0,0)\text{ twice}&0&0&\pi^0,\eta_8
\end{array}}
$$
The table illustrates the <meson octet> of <pseudoscalar mesons>; the same <weight> calculation applies to another <meson> species arranged in the same <representation>. Here <flavour hypercharge> is $Y=B+S$, with $B$ the <baryon number> and $S$ the <strangeness>, rather than <weak hypercharge>. If the full <quark model> <tensor product> $\mathbf3\otimes\overline{\mathbf3}$ is retained, its additional <trivial representation> is a ninth <meson> with $(q,y)=(0,0)$. This singlet is separate from the octet and need not share its <mass>.

= c
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Order the <simple roots> of the <G2 root system> with $\alpha_1$ long and $\alpha_2$ short. This ordering is consistent with the supplied projection in <3/d/solution>; reversing the nodes would interchange the two fundamental <Dynkin labels>. Choose $(\alpha_1,\alpha_1)=2$, $(\alpha_2,\alpha_2)=2/3$, and $(\alpha_1,\alpha_2)=-1$. With the same row convention for the <Cartan matrix> as in <3/a/solution>,
$$
\boxed{A_{G_2}=\begin{pmatrix}2&-3\\-1&2\end{pmatrix},\qquad
\alpha_1=(2,-3),\quad\alpha_2=(-1,2).}
$$
The <positive roots>, with their <Dynkin labels>, are
$$
\begin{array}{c|c|c}
\text{root}&\text{Dynkin labels}&\text{length type}\\\hline
\alpha_2&(-1,2)&\text{short}\\
\alpha_1+\alpha_2&(1,-1)&\text{short}\\
\alpha_1+2\alpha_2&(0,1)&\text{short}\\
\alpha_1&(2,-3)&\text{long}\\
\alpha_1+3\alpha_2&(-1,3)&\text{long}\\
2\alpha_1+3\alpha_2&(1,0)&\text{long}
\end{array}
$$
For the <highest weight> $(0,1)$, a lowering chain is
$$
(0,1)\xrightarrow{-\alpha_2}(1,-1)
\xrightarrow{-\alpha_1}(-1,2)
\xrightarrow{-\alpha_2}(0,0)
\xrightarrow{-\alpha_2}(1,-2)
\xrightarrow{-\alpha_1}(-1,1)
\xrightarrow{-\alpha_2}(0,-1).
$$
The middle three terms form the length-three <weight string> for the short <simple root>. The <Weyl dimension formula>, with this long-first convention, is
$$
\dim V(a,b)=\frac{(a+1)(b+1)(a+b+2)(2a+b+3)(3a+b+4)(3a+2b+5)}{120}.
$$
It gives seven for $(0,1)$, so the chain exhausts the <weight system>, with every <weight multiplicity> equal to one:
$$
\boxed{\mathbf7=(0,1):\quad(0,0),\quad
\pm(0,1),\quad\pm(1,-1),\quad\pm(-1,2).}
$$
For $(1,0)$, observe that $2\alpha_1+3\alpha_2=(1,0)$ is the <highest root>. Thus this is the <adjoint representation>; the twelve <root spaces> contribute the twelve nonzero <weights> once, and the rank-two <Cartan subalgebra> contributes two zero <weights>. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\mathbf{14}=(1,0):\quad
\pm(2,-3),\ \pm(-1,3),\ \pm(1,0),\
\pm(-1,2),\ \pm(1,-1),\ \pm(0,1),\ (0,0)^{[2]}.}
$$
The same <Weyl dimension formula> gives fourteen, confirming the count.

= d
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The <highest root> is $\theta=2\alpha_1+3\alpha_2$. In the <extended Dynkin diagram>, the extra node $-\theta$ is long and is joined by a single bond to the long node $\alpha_1$; it is orthogonal to the short node $\alpha_2$. Deleting the short node therefore leaves the <A2 root system> <Dynkin diagram>. Equivalently, the six long <roots> are a closed <root subsystem>, so their <root spaces> together with the <Cartan subalgebra> form an <A2 root system> <Lie subalgebra>. The corresponding compact <subgroup> is $SU(3)\subset G_2$.

For the particular projection in the paper, choose the <A2 root system> <simple roots> as $\beta_1=\alpha_1+3\alpha_2$ and $\beta_2=\alpha_1$. Since these are long, $\beta_1^\vee=\alpha_1^\vee+\alpha_2^\vee$ and $\beta_2^\vee=\alpha_1^\vee$. Consequently the <Dynkin labels> restrict as
$$
\boxed{(a,b)^T\longmapsto P(a,b)^T=(a+b,a)^T,\qquad
P=\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\1&0\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
Projecting the six short <roots> gives $\pm(1,0),\pm(0,1),\pm(1,-1)$. Group these into the three <weights> of $\mathbf3$ and the three of $\overline{\mathbf3}$ listed in <3/a/solution>. The zero <weight> of $\mathbf7$ is left over, so
$$
\boxed{\mathbf7\downarrow_{SU(3)}=\mathbf3\oplus\overline{\mathbf3}\oplus\mathbf1.}
$$
The three positive long <roots> project as $(2,-3)\mapsto(-1,2)$, $(-1,3)\mapsto(2,-1)$ and $(1,0)\mapsto(1,1)$. Together with their negatives and the two zero <weights>, these are exactly the <adjoint representation> $\mathbf8$. The remaining six short <roots> again give $\mathbf3\oplus\overline{\mathbf3}$. Hence the <branching of a representation> is
$$
\boxed{\mathbf{14}\downarrow_{SU(3)}=\mathbf8\oplus\mathbf3\oplus\overline{\mathbf3}.}
$$

= e
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

A <G2 (mathematics)> <multiplet> containing the known <meson octet> must restrict to a <representation> containing $\mathbf8$. Among the two fundamental <representations> just computed, $\mathbf7$ fails this test, while the <adjoint representation> $\mathbf{14}$ contains the required octet. Thus the minimal such choice is
$$
\boxed{\text{meson octet}\subset\mathbf{14}=(1,0).}
$$
Larger <representations> containing an octet would be other model choices; the octet alone does not force a unique arbitrarily large <representation>.

A dual <vector> must be pulled back by $P^T$, since $q_A^TPw=(P^Tq_A)^Tw$. Applying this to the <electric charge> and <flavour hypercharge> <vectors> gives
$$
\boxed{Q_{G_2}=P^T\frac13\begin{pmatrix}2\\1\end{pmatrix}
=\begin{pmatrix}1\\2/3\end{pmatrix},\qquad
Y_{G_2}=P^T\frac13\begin{pmatrix}1\\2\end{pmatrix}
=\begin{pmatrix}1\\1/3\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
Thus a <G2 root system> <weight> $(a,b)$ has $(q,y)=(a+2b/3,a+b/3)$. This pullback ensures that every known octet <meson> retains its values from <3/b/solution>. The extra $\mathbf3\oplus\overline{\mathbf3}$ gives six additional <mesons>, one at each of
$$
\boxed{(q,y)=\left(\tfrac23,\tfrac13\right),\
\left(-\tfrac13,\tfrac13\right),\
\left(-\tfrac13,-\tfrac23\right),\
\left(-\tfrac23,-\tfrac13\right),\
\left(\tfrac13,-\tfrac13\right),\
\left(\tfrac13,\tfrac23\right).}
$$
For instance the first three come from the <G2 root system> <weights> $(0,1)$, $(1,-2)$ and $(-1,1)$; the other three come from their negatives. The <weight diagram> displays the preserved octet and the new states.

\Image[2021-g2-meson-charges.png]
{title=The meson octet and its minimal G2 extension in electric charge and flavour hypercharge coordinates}

The fractional <electric charges> make these additional states qualitatively different from ordinary <quark>-<antiquark> <mesons>, whose <electric charges> are integers. This is a consequence of the proposed <symmetry> extension, not evidence for such particles. If “known mesons” also includes a flavour singlet from $\mathbf3\otimes\overline{\mathbf3}$, that singlet can be accommodated by a separate <G2 (mathematics)> <trivial representation>: the minimal octet-plus-singlet construction is then $\mathbf{14}\oplus\mathbf1$, still with six new states. A demand for one irreducible <G2 (mathematics)> <multiplet> containing the entire nonet is a different extra assumption; $\mathbf{14}$ alone does not contain that singlet.

